<resources>
    <!-- Enabling the auxiliary cameras lists camera 2/3/4 in the lens selector.

         media_profiles_kera_v1.xml declares 2160p frameRate=60 for cameraId 2/3/4 only;
         cameraId 0 and 1 have no 60 fps entry in any section. Those entries are what make
         Aperture offer a 60 fps option at all, since it derives the frame rates it offers
         from the media profiles.

         Cost, as the AOSP documentation for this flag states: logical lens switching on
         that side is ignored, so the lens is selected explicitly instead, which is what
         the added 2/3/4 entries are for. -->
    <bool name="config_enableAuxCameras">true</bool>
    <!-- An array of triplets made of (camera ID, approximate zoom ratio, exact zoom ratio).
         These values will be added to the lens selector if one or more cameras uses the
         multi-camera API to let the user select which lens to use.
         It's highly recommended to leave config_enableAuxCameras to false, since with
         multi-camera API you only use a single camera device for all sensors.
         You may enable it if you need auxiliary camera devices for a set of cameras on one
         of the device's side (e.g. logical main back camera but multiple physical camera
         devices on front), in that case if a main device side's lens is referenced here
         all the auxiliary camera devices of that side will be ignored.
         You may be able to get the exact zoom ratio needed to trigger the lens switch from
         your stock camera app and observing the zoom ratio it sets with the following command:
         adb shell dumpsys media.camera | grep 'android.control.zoomRatio' -1
         Example for Google Pixel 7 Pro's back camera configuration:
             <string-array name="config_logicalZoomRatios">
                 <item>0</item> <item>0.5</item> <item>0.55632502</item>
                 <item>0</item> <item>2.0</item> <item>2.0</item>
                 <item>0</item> <item>5.0</item> <item>5.0</item>
             </string-array>
    -->
    <string-array name="config_logicalZoomRatios">
        <item>0</item> <item>0.6</item> <item>0.6</item>
        <item>0</item> <item>1.0</item> <item>1.0</item>
        <item>0</item> <item>2.0</item> <item>2.0</item>
        <item>0</item> <item>3.5</item> <item>3.5</item>
        <item>0</item> <item>7.0</item> <item>7.0</item>
        <!-- Front camera (1). The lens selector only lists presets for cameras named here, so
             without these the front camera has no zoom buttons at all. The camera reports a
             maximum digital zoom of 8.0, so these stay inside its range. -->
        <item>1</item> <item>1.0</item> <item>1.0</item>
        <item>1</item> <item>2.0</item> <item>2.0</item>
        <item>1</item> <item>4.0</item> <item>4.0</item>
    </string-array>

    <!-- An array of triplets made of (camera ID, qualities, frame rates).
         These video modes will be added to the available
         quality/frame rate combinations of a camera device.
         Make sure the device is able to handle those configurations
         and maintain a stable frame rate at any condition.
         Note that you can't add video qualities that aren't
         exposed by the camera, only new frame rates.
         Valid values of resolution are:
          - "sd" (480p)
          - "hd" (720p)
          - "fhd" (1080p)
          - "uhd" (2160p)
         Valid values of frame rate are:
          - "24"
          - "30"
          - "60"
          - "120"
         Additionally you can prepend `-` to frame rate to mark it as unsupported.
         Example:
             <string-array name="config_additionalVideoConfigurations">
                 <item>0</item> <item>sd|hd|fhd</item> <item>60|120</item>
                 <item>0</item> <item>uhd</item> <item>-60</item>
                 <item>1</item> <item>sd|hd|fhd</item> <item>60</item>
             </string-array>
    -->
    <!-- An entry that declared "camera 0 supports hd|fhd@60" was removed. It read:

             <item>0</item> <item>hd|fhd</item> <item>60</item>

         Its comment claimed camera 0's HAL offers high-speed 1920x1080@30-120, making
         1080p60 possible. That was not reproduced: the cameraId 0 section of the vendor
         media profile declares no 60 fps entry, and no recording made from this tree shows
         a 1080p60 attempt. 1080p60 is therefore not advertised here.

         This does not mean camera 0 cannot record at 60 fps. Measured on this device, the
         logical back camera (cameraId 0) records 3840x2160 at about 60 fps on the ordinary
         recording path with no physical sub-camera routing (CamX configure_streams reports
         an empty physical_camera_id, the session runs on CameraId-0, and camera requests
         carry aeTargetFpsRange=[60,60]); the encoder profile resolved for it is the cameraId
         0 2160p entry, which itself declares frameRate 30. A stream's actual rate comes from
         the HAL vendor frame-rate tables, not from the profile's frameRate attribute. This
         array therefore stays empty. -->
    <string-array name="config_additionalVideoConfigurations" />
</resources>
